## Is incontinence a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Incontinence that has a substantial, long-term effect on daily life can be covered, especially when linked to another condition.

## At work

- easy access to a **toilet**, and toilet breaks when you need them
- somewhere private to change products
- a desk near the toilet, and flexible hours for appointments

## Benefits

PIP includes an activity called **managing toilet needs or incontinence**. You may score points if, on most days, you need an aid, such as pads, or help from another person to manage incontinence.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
